--TEST--
A memory-limit breach inside `try { … } finally { … }` is `rule:errors/on-limit`'s clean `FATAL`, and the `finally` does not run — `rule:errors/on-limit`, `rule:errors/propagation`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:errors/fatal-errors-limits-no-catch-sees
// `rule:errors/on-limit`'s breach is a `FATAL`, so it reaches no `catch` and no
// `finally`: every copy of a `finally` body lives behind a landing block's
// catchable exit, and `nvs_ir::ir::Terminator::Catch` admits a `THROWN` alone.
// What this case is really written around is the *other* exit — the one that
// releases the frame on its way out — and a `foreach` inside the region is what
// makes it interesting. The loop's reserved `foreach#N` binding holds a second
// reference to the array being walked and exists on only some of the region's
// landing edges, so the region's dispatch drops it and the drop owes a release
// on the way into the handler. That release goes into a block of the catchable
// exit's own (`Lowering::landing_block`), not into the landing block both exits
// leave through, so the uncatchable exit does not release the same reference a
// second time and the run prints the two lines below.
echo "start\n";

// Under the 16M grant on its own, and past it by the third slab: the file's
// ceiling is 16777216 with 1048576 of it reserved for `rule:errors/on-limit`'s tier-1
// handler, so two 8 MiB slabs are already over what ordinary execution may
// hold and the third `Core\Str::repeat` is the member that reports it.
array<string> $tags = ["a", "b", "c", "d"];
try {
    array<string> $ballast = [];
    foreach ($tags as string $tag) {
        $ballast[] = Core\Str::repeat($tag, 8388608);
    }
    // Only reachable in a tree where the ceiling was never enforced, and here
    // for that reason: it is also the second landing edge of this region, the
    // one binding no `foreach#N`, which is what makes the dispatch drop that
    // name at all.
    echo "held=", Core\Arr::count($ballast), "\n";
} finally {
    // `rule:errors/on-limit`: a resource limit is not an exception, so this never runs.
    echo "finally\n";
}
echo "after\n";
--FILE nvs.toml--
# `[limits] memory` is the whole of what this case configures — the grant the
# ballast above is written to exceed on its third slab. `rule:config/ini-set-is-core-config-set` is the unit.
[limits]
memory = "16M"
--EXPECT--
start
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
FATAL: the program exceeded its memory limit — %d bytes held against a ceiling of 16777216, of which 1048576 is reserved for the limit handler, when an allocation of %d bytes was refused
help: raise `memory` under `[limits]` in nvs.toml, or call `Core\Config::set('memory', '32M')` in the program
